window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Week 1 Content","Week 2 Content","Not Tested","A ★ marks something the instructor said outright would be on an exam."]},{"s":"Week 1 Content▾","a":"week-1-content","k":["Week 1 Content▾","First Sub-Topic","★ A thing she said you will be tested on — stated in about twenty words, with the specific number or threshold included.","A second point: high-yield, no star, because she never said it would be tested.","Second Sub-Topic","Group related facts into one bullet rather than splitting them — a bullet that carries a definition, its threshold, and its consequence is one reference item, not three.","Keep the wording tight. Drop the mechanism asides, the percentages you won't be asked for, and \"she said…\" on any bullet that already has a star.","Quick Reference Table","A compact table for material the instructor said to know exhaustively — the kind of list that would be twenty bullets otherwise.","First item — What lets you recognize it, including the discriminating test. — What is given. — What happens if it isn't.","Second item — Four columns is about the practical limit for readability. — Keep cells short. — One clause each."]},{"s":"Week 2 Content▾","a":"week-2-content","k":["Week 2 Content▾","Sub-Topic","Copy a .hint-group block per sub-topic, and an h2.block per week."]},{"s":"Not Tested▾","a":"not-tested","k":["Not Tested▾","Explicitly Excluded","Things the instructor said you do not need to memorize — exact doses, specific percentages, brand names, and so on.","Record these the moment you hear them. They are the cheapest studying you will ever skip."]}]},{"f":"week1-respiratory-labs-diagnostics.html","t":"Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","How Lab Results Are Obtained","Pulse Oximetry","Sputum Tests","Imaging","TB Testing","Bronchoscopy","Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One","Thoracentesis","Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)","Self-Test Flashcards","Every body system in Med-Surg gets its own labs-and-diagnostics lecture first. Related nursing care — pre- and post-procedure — is fair game on exams."]},{"s":"How Lab Results Are Obtained▾","a":"how-lab-results-are-obtained","k":["How Lab Results Are Obtained▾","Venous draw — most standard labs.","Fingerstick — most blood glucose checks.","Arterial draw — used for an ABG (arterial blood gas). At UK this is typically done by the respiratory therapist, not the nurse.","ABGs are respiratory content, but this course saves ABG interpretation for block two — it isn't covered in depth on this page."]},{"s":"Pulse Oximetry▾","a":"pulse-oximetry","k":["Pulse Oximetry▾","MNEMONIC: think of oxygen delivery like a bus system. The red blood cell is the bus; hemoglobin (built from iron) is the seats; O2 is the passengers sitting in those seats. Pulse ox / O2 sat = how full the buses are. Hematocrit = the total …","Normal pulse ox / O2 sat: ≥ 95%.","A low O2 sat can point to hypoventilation, atelectasis, a pneumothorax, or other lung issues.","Non-invasive; can be intermittent (with routine vitals, e.g. Q4) or continuous (common right after surgery).","Commonly used to titrate oxygen levels in hospitalized patients.","Best site is the fingertip — remove nail polish first. The earlobe and toe can be used, but the finger is what the sensor is actually made for."]},{"s":"Sputum Tests▾","a":"sputum-tests","k":["Sputum Tests▾","Culture & Sensitivity (C&S) — Whether there's bacteria in the sputum, and what it's sensitive to. — Guides antibiotic choice.","Cytology — Cancer cells — ordered when lung cancer is suspected. — Part of the lung cancer diagnostic workup.","Acid-Fast Bacillus (AFB) — Active tuberculosis. — Confirms whether latent TB has converted to active/reactivated TB.","Collect a deep specimen (not saliva) in a sterile container and get it to the lab right away — don't let it sit at the bedside. The morning specimen is best: secretions are more concentrated after a night of lying still, and the patient has…"]},{"s":"Imaging▾","a":"imaging","k":["Imaging▾","Chest X-Ray — A 2-D snapshot of the lung. Most common views: PA and lateral. — Patient removes metal between neck and waist (bra straps, necklaces).","CT (CAT) Scan — Cross-sectional structures — a 3-D-style picture built from slices. — May require sedation (patient must lie still, or if claustrophobic). Loud clicking noise — warn the patient. Contrast is provider preference and is iodine…","MRI — Assesses lesions that are hard to see on CT; distinguishes vascular from non-vascular structures. — Remove all metal. May need sedation for claustrophobia in a closed MRI. Contrast used is not iodine-based.","PET Scan — Physiology and function (blood flow, sugar uptake) rather than just structure — uses an injected radioactive tracer. — Used heavily for cancer diagnosis, and for some brain disorders (Alzheimer's, epilepsy, brain tumors).","CT with contrast — check renal function first. Contrast is nephrotoxic. Check creatinine (kidney-specific) and BUN before the scan, and notify the provider if either is elevated. Patient should be encouraged to drink fluids afterward to hel…","A shellfish allergy is not actually linked to contrast reactions — the real concern is an iodine allergy. Some standardized tests (including the NCLEX) can still ask about it the old way, so know it for boards even though it's outdated in p…","Expect a patient getting IV contrast to feel a warm flush spread through their body — that's normal, not a sign of a reaction, but tell them beforehand so it doesn't alarm them."]},{"s":"TB Testing▾","a":"tb-testing","k":["TB Testing▾","TB Skin Test (Mantoux)","Injected intradermally, at only a 10–15° angle — just enough to raise a bleb under the skin.","Must be read in person at a follow-up visit.","IGRA (blood test)","Interferon Gamma Release Assay — a blood draw instead of a skin test.","No need to return to have it read.","Neither option is better than the other — they're just two different ways to get the same answer."]},{"s":"Bronchoscopy▾","a":"bronchoscopy","k":["Bronchoscopy▾","Scopes the bronchi — used to visualize the airway and/or obtain a biopsy sample (e.g. secretions the patient can't expectorate on their own).","Consent signed beforehand — It's an invasive procedure.","NPO 6–12 hours before — Reduces nausea/vomiting and aspiration risk once sedated.","Sedation given; oral pharynx anesthetized — So the patient doesn't gag or vomit around the scope.","Keep NPO after the procedure until the gag reflex returns — The throat is numbed by the anesthetic used during the procedure.","Blood-tinged mucus after the procedure = expected finding — Minor trauma from the scope. Document and continue to monitor — no need to notify the provider for this alone.","Watch for bleeding and pneumothorax after any bronchoscopy or lung biopsy. An unexplained increased heart rate can signal hemorrhaging; abrupt shortness of breath can signal a pneumothorax (collapsed lung). Notify the provider."]},{"s":"Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One▾","a":"lung-biopsy-four-ways-to-get-one","k":["Lung Biopsy — Four Ways to Get One▾","Transbronchial Lung Biopsy — Via bronchoscopy — a piece of tissue is taken through the scope. — Endoscopy suite.","Trans-Thoracic Needle Aspiration — A needle is put directly through the thorax, with CT guidance. — Radiology, with CT guidance.","Open Lung Biopsy — Provider goes in surgically. — OR.","VATS (Video-Assisted Thoracic Surgery) — Video-assisted, through the thorax. — OR.","\"Scoping\" always refers to going in through a natural orifice with a camera — a bronchoscopy scopes the bronchus the way a colonoscopy scopes the colon or a cystoscopy scopes the bladder. Endoscopy is the umbrella term for all of them."]},{"s":"Thoracentesis▾","a":"thoracentesis","k":["Thoracentesis▾","A large-bore needle removes fluid (or air) from the pleural space — most often done for a pleural effusion, since fluid there reduces the surface area available for oxygen delivery and can cause respiratory problems.","Consent signed — Invasive procedure.","Position: sitting upright, leaning forward, elbows on an overhead table — Pulling the elbows toward the overhead table opens up the intercostal spaces the provider needs to access.","Instruct the patient not to talk during the procedure — Patient needs to stay very still.","Chest X-ray after the procedure — Confirms the provider didn't inadvertently nick the pleura and cause a pneumothorax.","Assess for signs of hypoxia after — Same reason — ruling out a pneumothorax.","A paracentesis is the equivalent procedure for fluid collected in the peritoneum (covered later, in hepatobiliary) — same idea, different space. Fluid sitting somewhere it doesn't belong and serves no purpose is called third spacing."]},{"s":"Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)▾","a":"pulmonary-function-tests-pfts","k":["Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)▾","Total Lung Capacity — Inhale as deep as possible, then exhale as deep as possible. — The full range of lung volume, not just a normal breath.","FEV1 (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 Second) — Patient takes a deep breath in, then pushes the air out as fast as possible — how much comes out in that first second. — Patients with obstructive pulmonary disease (e.g. COPD) have trouble gett…","Peak Flow Meter — A small device the patient carries and blows into themselves to check their own FEV1. — Mainly an asthma-monitoring tool (covered in patho-pharm) — a falling number relative to the patient's personal best can signal an ear…","PFTs are performed by a respiratory therapist: the patient's nose is pinched, mouth sealed around the device, breathing in and out on command."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-upper-respiratory-problems.html","t":"Upper Respiratory Problems","secs":[{"s":"Upper Respiratory Problems","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","Epistaxis (Nosebleed)","Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)","Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)","Acute Pharyngitis","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Epistaxis (Nosebleed)▾","a":"epistaxis-nosebleed","k":["Epistaxis (Nosebleed)▾","Epistaxis is just the clinical name for a nosebleed.","Common Causes","Dry air (irritated nasal mucosa) — including going somewhere with drier air, like the mountains.","Trauma, infection, or a foreign body (think pediatric patients).","Tumors.","Hypertension.","A blood disorder / blood dyscrasia — e.g. a patient on anticoagulants with thin blood, or someone who's had chemotherapy.","If a patient has a spontaneous nosebleed, ask whether they're on anticoagulants — it likely explains the cause, and affects how quickly you can get it to stop.","IV Heparin — Protamine sulfate.","Warfarin — Vitamin K.","Anterior Nosebleed","The one you'd typically think of — up in the front part of the nose.","Position the patient upright, leaning forward (not backward — blood going down the back of the throat could cause aspiration).","Lateral pressure on the nose, with ice, to vasoconstrict.","Discourage nose blowing — especially important if the patient has increased ICP, since blowing increases pressure in the nose.","Pinch just below the bony prominence, on the soft tissue — pinching the bone itself won't do any good.","Posterior Nosebleed","A bigger deal — usually requires hospitalization.","Hard to stop: you can't pinch bleeding happening at the back of the bone.","May need a balloon catheter or posterior packing (a tamponade — pressure applied to obstruct the capillaries and stop the bleed).","With packing in place, keep an eye on respiratory status (it can impede breathing) — humidification, oxygenation, bed rest, pain control, oral care, and patient teaching (e.g. saline spray).","Avoid aspirin and NSAIDs (they thin the blood) and avoid strenuous activity.","A posterior nosebleed is an emergency. It's difficult to control and usually requires hospitalization."]},{"s":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)▾","a":"obstructive-sleep-apnea-osa","k":["Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)▾","Apnea = a cessation of breathing. Hypopnea = breathing slows considerably (both are respiratory-effort-related).","Caused by repetitive collapsing of the upper airway during sleep — the mandible drops back and blocks the airway, and the patient isn't aware it's happening.","Obesity — A lot of adipose tissue and a large neck circumference literally impedes the airway by pressing on it.","Nasal/Pharyngeal Structural Abnormalities — —","Increased Age — —","Male Sex — —","Smoking — ★ Also a risk factor, but a less well-established one than the four above.","Signs and symptoms: patients are often unaware they have it — they're just sleepy, and it's usually their significant other who reports the snoring/noises. Also: morning headaches (from lack of quality sleep), a large neck/waist circumferen…","Polysomnography (a sleep study, done at a sleep center) is the gold standard for diagnosing OSA.","CPAP","Continuous Positive Airway Pressure — the mainstay treatment for OSA, and what's used most often.","Simple, works well, and cheap.","One constant pressure the whole time; no supplemental oxygen involved (though it may be added at home).","Pushes positive pressure into the airway to help it stay open on both inhalation and exhalation.","BiPAP","A little fancier and more expensive than CPAP; non-invasive (as opposed to the BiPAP used in the ICU).","Two pressures: one during inhalation, a lower one during exhalation — easier for the patient to push air out.","Works well for a patient who has trouble exhaling air, e.g. COPD.","Also used for neuromuscular disease and chest wall deformities.","One of the last steps before intubation — trying to prevent having to intubate and send the patient to the ICU.","An estimated 20–40% of patients with a CPAP/BiPAP order don't actually use it, because it's uncomfortable — ask about this and look for patient education opportunities. Other treatments: weight reduction, exercise, avoiding alcohol and smok…"]},{"s":"Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)▾","a":"tracheostomy-care-revisit","k":["Tracheostomy Care (Revisit)▾","A quick revisit, since trachs come up in med-surg — this is fair game for clinical and for exams.","Phalange — the plate on the outside.","Outer cannula — goes into the stoma; the inner cannula goes inside the outer cannula.","Obturator — used to reinsert a trach that's been accidentally coughed out. It has a point that helps ease entry into the stoma.","Shiley (plastic, white)","Has a cuff (can be inflated or not).","What a patient gets initially, fresh post-op — even if the trach will end up being long-term.","Disposable inner cannula — thrown away and replaced at trach care, not scrubbed.","Jackson (metal)","No cuff.","Switched to once the patient has had the trach long enough to be long-term.","Inner cannula is cleaned with a scrub brush, not thrown away.","Either type still needs its obturator taped to the bedside at all times.","Extend the patient's neck — Straightens the neck out to open the tissue.","Insert the obturator into the outer cannula, then insert into the stoma — The obturator's point facilitates entry into the stoma.","Remove the obturator — Leaving it in would obstruct the airway.","Reinsert the inner cannula — Completes the trach.","Check breath sounds and secure the trach — Confirms placement and keeps it from coming back out."]},{"s":"Acute Pharyngitis▾","a":"acute-pharyngitis","k":["Acute Pharyngitis▾","\"-itis\" always means inflammation — pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharynx and tonsils.","Viral — The most common type by far.","Bacterial (Streptococcal) — Only about 1 in 10 adults with a sore throat actually have bacterial pharyngitis. Sudden-onset sore throat, tonsillar hypertrophy (swollen, red, erythematous, tender tonsils), lymphadenopathy, and fever.","Fungal — Seen in patients on antibiotics — commonly called thrush, caused by candida albicans.","Viral and bacterial pharyngitis are difficult to tell apart by symptoms alone — a rapid strep test determines which one it is.","Only bacterial pharyngitis needs antibiotics. A patient with viral pharyngitis should not be put on antibiotics — inappropriate use drives antibiotic resistance.","Bacterial — Antibiotics.","Fungal (Thrush) — Nystatin — a swish-and-spit or swish-and-swallow solution.","Viral — Symptomatic care only: soothing treatments, analgesics, anti-pyretics."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-head-neck-cancer.html","t":"Head & Neck Cancer","secs":[{"s":"Head & Neck Cancer","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Risk Factors & Presentation","Treatment","Laryngectomy — What Changes","Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy","Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care","Self-Test Flashcards","What makes head & neck cancer so devastating is as much about the physical modification as anything else — a patient can lose their mandible, tongue, or larynx, which affects body image as well as function. And the airway runs right through…"]},{"s":"Risk Factors & Presentation▾","a":"risk-factors-presentation","k":["Risk Factors & Presentation▾","Tobacco use causes 85% of head & neck cancers.","More common in men than women.","Most often diagnosed at > 50 years old.","If the patient is < 50, it's often associated with HPV (human papillomavirus) instead.","Most patients already have locally advanced disease by the time they're diagnosed."]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Surgery is the first line of treatment. Radiation and chemotherapy are also used, but the cancer-specific details of those are covered later, in oncology — not in this lecture.","Vocal Cord Stripping — A less extensive option than removing the larynx entirely.","Laryngectomy — Removal of the larynx (the voice box) — \"-ectomy\" means removal.","Tracheostomy — Often follows a laryngectomy — \"-ostomy\" means opening.","Lymph Node Removal / Neck Dissection — Done if the cancer has metastasized or spread into the muscle — may involve dissecting neck muscles like the sternocleidomastoid. Can be an extensive surgery depending on how far the cancer has spread."]},{"s":"Laryngectomy — What Changes▾","a":"laryngectomy-what-changes","k":["Laryngectomy — What Changes▾","Worth pausing on, because it's simple but easy to overlook: normally, food goes down the esophagus, and air comes in through the mouth or nose and down the trachea. After a laryngectomy, the larynx (and the connection between the airway and…","Eating is still possible — the GI tract connection (esophagus) is intact. There may be a temporary tube feeding while post-op swelling heals, but eating normally is the eventual goal.","Air now enters only through the stoma — never again through the nose or mouth.","When the patient coughs, sputum comes out through the stoma — nothing comes out of the nose or mouth, because they're no longer connected to the airway.","In a code situation, bag the stoma — not the mouth or nose. A patient with a laryngectomy no longer takes air in through their mouth or nose. Use a pediatric adapter (a smaller face mask that fits over the stoma) to bag them."]},{"s":"Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy▾","a":"restoring-communication-after-a-laryngectomy","k":["Restoring Communication After a Laryngectomy▾","Artificial Larynx — A handheld device placed against the throat that provides vibration to produce speech. — Easy to use, can be used immediately after surgery — the most common option today. Can sound a bit robotic, but understandable.","Tracheoesophageal Voice Restoration — A valve device placed surgically that lets the patient talk. — —","Esophageal Speech — The patient sucks air into the esophagus, then forms words while \"burping\" the air back out. — Hands-free (no device needed) — but hard to learn, and can be difficult to understand depending on the patient's skill with i…"]},{"s":"Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care▾","a":"radical-neck-dissection-post-op-care","k":["Radical Neck Dissection & Post-Op Care▾","A radical neck dissection removes part of the neck muscles — and possibly salivary glands or major blood vessels — because of how far the cancer has spread. It can be disfiguring; part of the neck may visibly cave in where tissue was remove…","Airway patency — priority #1 — There's a lot going on in the neck after this kind of surgery.","Humidify the trach collar / O2 — Normally the nose and mouth humidify air before it reaches the lungs; a new trach bypasses that, so add humidification until the patient adjusts to breathing without it.","Get the patient up and coughing regularly; suction as needed — Keeps the airway clear.","Blood-tinged sputum in the first couple of days = expected finding — Trauma from the surgery itself.","Pulmonary toilet: manage secretions, stoma care, and pain — Keeps the patient comfortable enough to participate in their own recovery.","Start tube feedings right away (sometimes even before surgery) — These patients are often malnourished pre-op (haven't felt like eating) — good nutrition enhances healing.","Physical therapy and speech therapy, post-operatively — —","Blood-tinged secretions as an expected post-procedure finding comes up again here — same principle as blood-tinged mucus after a bronchoscopy, review in Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics if needed"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-lower-respiratory-problems.html","t":"Lower Respiratory Problems","secs":[{"s":"Lower Respiratory Problems","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","Pneumonia","COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)","Tuberculosis (TB)","Lung Cancer","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Pneumonia▾","a":"pneumonia","k":["Pneumonia▾","Pneumonia is an acute infection of the lung parenchyma — the functional unit of the lung (e.g. the alveoli, where O2/CO2 exchange happens).","Risk Factors","Older age.","An altered level of consciousness or a weakened cough — either can put a patient at risk for aspiration pneumonia, since they can't effectively expectorate secretions.","Immobility / prolonged bed rest, a debilitating illness, malnutrition, and smoking.","Tracheal intubation — a patient on a ventilator in the ICU (this is why VAP is an ICU concept, not med-surg).","CAP (Community-Acquired) — Occurs in people who haven't been hospitalized, or who've been in a long-term care facility for <14 days of symptoms.","HAP (Hospital-Acquired) — Begins 48+ hours after admission — not present at the time of admission.","VAP (Ventilator-Associated) — HAP that develops in an intubated ICU patient on a ventilator. If the patient isn't intubated, it's classified as HAP instead.","Viral — The most common type. Usually mild/self-limiting (resolves in 3–4 days on its own) — but can still cause life-threatening problems.","Bacterial — Usually more serious than viral, and often requires hospitalization.","Aspiration — Abnormal entry of material from the mouth or stomach into the trachea/lungs, where it doesn't belong. Risk factors: decreased LOC, dysphagia (thin liquids are especially risky), and NG/feeding tubes. Can be silent — no vomiting…","Opportunistic — Occurs in immunocompromised patients (e.g. HIV) — covered further when opportunistic diseases come up later.","For a patient with an NG/feeding tube, placement matters: those tiny yellow feeding tubes (\"dobhoff\" tubes) should sit post-pyloric (past the pyloric sphincter). If a tube migrates back up out of the stomach and up the esophagus, the tube f…","In an elderly patient, watch for new-onset confusion as an early sign of infection — including pneumonia. It's often one of the first things you'll notice, sometimes before the more \"typical\" symptoms show up (the same pattern comes up agai…","Signs & Symptoms","Fever, chills, cough, malaise.","Pain with deep breaths and coughing — so the patient may avoid taking good deep breaths, which becomes its own problem.","Dyspnea (shortness of air), fatigue, myalgia (achy muscles).","Bacterial pneumonia: typically a productive, purulent cough. Viral: usually scanty or non-productive.","Severe cases can progress to respiratory distress and respiratory failure.","MNEMONIC: Leukocytosis = an increase in white blood cells (leukocytes) — seen with an acute bacterial infection. Leukopenia = a decrease. Easy to mix up on an exam — worth committing to memory on its own.","Chest X-ray shows infiltrates — Haziness where the film should be clear — suggests infection (e.g. in the left lower lobe).","CBC with diff shows leukocytosis, with a \"shift to the left\" — An increase in neutrophils — and specifically an increase in immature neutrophils (the mature ones normally circulate; immature ones get released early to help fight an acute ba…","Sputum culture & sensitivity is positive — Identifies the bacteria and which antibiotics will kill it. A viral infection won't grow any bacteria on culture — helps differentiate viral from bacterial.","Prevention & Nursing Care","Pneumococcal vaccine for anyone at risk or of the recommended age; don't smoke; balanced diet.","For a hospitalized patient: identify aspiration risk and act on it (positioning, diet texture, tube placement).","Turn, cough, deep breathe; ambulate; pulmonary mobilization — keeps secretions moving.","Strict hand hygiene and standard precautions.","Monitor vital signs and pulse ox regularly, and trend the data — a new fever or a dropping O2 sat is a meaningful change.","Listen to lung sounds at the start of your shift to set a baseline — so you can tell whether new crackles a few hours later are actually new.","Supplemental O2 as needed to keep pulse ox > 95%; incentive spirometer (very few contraindications — most patients should be using one, even if it's just sitting unused by the window); chest physiotherapy (percussion/postural drainage) to h…","Increase fluids to thin secretions, unless contraindicated (e.g. severe heart failure).","Energy conservation — bundle care for a weak patient with activity intolerance, but still get them up and moving.","Antibiotics if bacterial; analgesics/antipyretics for pain or fever; discharge teaching."]},{"s":"COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)▾","a":"copd-chronic-obstructive-pulmonary-disease","k":["COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease)▾","COPD is an umbrella term covering chronic bronchitis and emphysema (asthma is technically an obstructive disease too, but it's covered separately in patho-pharm, not here). Most patients with COPD have some of both — collectively, this cour…","Emphysema","Alveolar damage — the alveoli get overinflated/stretched and don't work as well.","Impaired gas exchange at the alveolar level (where O2/CO2 actually exchange) — this is the critical piece.","Chronic Bronchitis","Different pathology than emphysema — its key feature is excessive secretion production.","COPD is preventable and treatable. The most common cause of an exacerbation (a flare-up) is a respiratory infection — a patient might get a mild cold, and the next thing you know they've developed a full flare requiring hospitalization. Dia…","Exam Findings","Barrel chest — years of working hard to exhale expands the thorax; the AP diameter moves from the normal 2:1 ratio toward 1:1.","Decreased/diminished breath sounds, often with adventitious sounds — wheezing, rhonchi, crackles (especially during an exacerbation).","Prolonged expiration — it takes longer to get the air out, because (unlike a normal passive exhale) they have to push it out.","Pursed-lip breathing in advanced disease — purses the lips while blowing air out, creating positive pressure between the lips and alveoli that helps push the trapped air out. It's a compensatory mechanism most patients learn on their own.","Advanced disease: neck vein distention and peripheral edema (from pulmonary hypertension — high blood pressure inside the lungs makes blood back up systemically) and cachexia (excessively malnourished — breathing simply takes an enormous nu…","Example from the lecture: a 63-year-old male with a history of COPD presented with 2 weeks of worsening cough, yellow sputum, and dyspnea; O2 sat was 87% in the ED. His chest X-ray showed pneumonia (infiltrates/consolidation), some pleural …","Nursing Problems to Anticipate","Activity intolerance — poor exercise tolerance; O2 sat may even drop while talking or eating.","Ineffective breathing pattern — too fast, too slow, too shallow, or too deep. Breathing too fast and deep is called hyperventilation.","Ineffective airway clearance — thick secretions the patient is too weak or too congested to cough up.","Impaired gas exchange — from the alveolar damage itself.","Anxiety — very common; patients can be jittery or agitated because they're having such a hard time breathing.","Poor nutritional status (see cachexia, above).","Smoking cessation — The single most effective, cost-effective intervention to reduce COPD risk and slow its progression.","Flu shot and other indicated vaccines — For high-risk patients.","Early detection/treatment of respiratory infections — Infections are the most common trigger for an exacerbation.","Inhaler therapy adherence — The mainstay of COPD self-management — patients who can't afford their medication or skip it are the ones who often end up hospitalized with an exacerbation.","Tripod positioning (leaning forward, hands on legs) — Expands the thorax and increases the surface area available for alveoli to exchange oxygen. Most patients adopt this on their own.","Pursed-lip breathing, huff coughing — Huff coughing: a series of small coughs followed by a hard cough, to help produce and expectorate secretions.","Energy conservation and relaxation techniques — —","Nutritional support — These patients burn an extraordinary number of calories just breathing.","Psychosocial support — Not being able to catch your breath is genuinely scary.","Long-Acting Beta Agonist (LABA) — Bronchodilates. A mainstay of treatment.","Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (LAMA) — Also bronchodilates.","Inhaled Steroids — Potent anti-inflammatories — decrease the inflammatory response and dilate the bronchi.","MDI (Metered Dose Inhaler)","What the patient is using at home, most of the time.","Nebulizer","Used when the patient is sick enough to be hospitalized.","Hooked up to a machine with humidification, with medication in it as well.","CO2 narcosis — never withhold oxygen from a hypoxic patient, even a \"CO2 retainer.\" A CO2 retainer is a COPD patient whose body has adapted, over a long period of time, to chronically high CO2 — so their stimulus to breathe becomes a low ox…","Exacerbation management: worsening dyspnea, cough, and sputum beyond the patient's typical day-to-day variation, requiring a change in treatment plan. The mainstay of treatment is oxygen to treat the hypoxemia — but always titrated with the…"]},{"s":"Tuberculosis (TB)▾","a":"tuberculosis-tb","k":["Tuberculosis (TB)▾","Caused by an infectious organism, Mycobacterium tuberculosis. If it's resistant to isoniazid (INH) and rifampin, it's called MDR-TB (multi-drug-resistant TB).","Risk Factors","Disproportionately affects poor, underserved, and minority populations.","IV drug users; people in crowded living situations or with less-than-ideal sanitation.","Immunosuppression, for any reason.","Primary TB — Bacteria is inhaled and triggers an inflammatory reaction; the immune system responds and encapsulates the organism — hopefully for the rest of the patient's life.","Latent TB — A persistent immune response to the bacterium, with no clinical manifestations of active disease. Asymptomatic and non-contagious — but will test positive on a skin or blood test. Can convert to active TB at any point, even year…","Active / Reactivated TB — Develops from latent TB in about 5–10% of patients, sometimes years afterward — risk is much higher in the elderly and immunosuppressed. This is the point at which the patient becomes contagious.","How the TB skin test and IGRA are actually done is covered on Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics — this page covers what the results mean, not how to give the test","Reading a TB skin test: come back 48–72 hours later and check for induration (a raised area) — not just redness (erythema). Redness alone isn't considered positive.","Healthy, no risk factors — ≥ 15 mm.","Higher-risk (immigrants, IV drug users, nursing home residents, young children) — ≥ 10 mm.","Immunosuppressed (HIV, recent contact with active TB, organ/lung transplant) — ≥ 5 mm.","If either the skin test or the IGRA is positive, the next step is a chest X-ray. A clear CXR (no Ghon nodule) means latent TB. A positive CXR (nodules noted) means the patient needs further testing to confirm active disease.","Isoniazid (INH) — Hepatotoxicity (along with acetaminophen — the two drugs in med-surg most associated with liver toxicity) and peripheral neuropathy (numbness/tingling in the fingers).","Rifampin — Turns excretions red or orange — including urine. Important to recognize this isn't hematuria (blood in the urine).","Ethambutol — Affects visual acuity and can cause difficulty differentiating between colors.","Strict adherence to drug therapy is essential — treatment for reactivated TB can run for months to years. For patients at high risk of non-adherence (e.g. a homeless patient), DOT (Direct Observational Therapy) means a provider directly sup…","Airborne precautions, negative-pressure room, HEPA mask — A negative-pressure room pushes air out rather than recirculating it to other patients' rooms — prevents aerosolized secretions from spreading. Staff must be fit-tested for the HEPA …","Home: prophylactic medication for high-risk household contacts — E.g. a TB-positive patient's spouse may also be started on medication.","Teach the patient to cover their mouth/nose when coughing and wear a mask in crowds — Reduces transmission.","Sputum for AFB every couple of weeks; 3 negative cultures = no longer infectious — Confirms the patient is no longer contagious with the reactivated version."]},{"s":"Lung Cancer▾","a":"lung-cancer","k":["Lung Cancer▾","One of the most prevalent lung cancers, particularly in Kentucky — worth its own voiceover. Often somewhat asymptomatic early on: 10% of the time it's discovered incidentally on an X-ray taken for some other reason. Symptoms are frequently …","Chest X-ray, CT, MRI, sputum for cytology, and the three ways to biopsy the lung are all covered on Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics — this page only covers what's specific to a lung cancer workup","Chest X-ray: may show a mass or an infiltrate (also called a consolidation, or just haziness).","CAT scan or MRI: used to assess for metastasis (spread).","Sputum for cytology: only about 20–30 out of 100 patients who actually have lung cancer will test positive on cytology alone.","A biopsy is the definitive diagnostic test — actual tissue, not just cells in the sputum.","Nursing Problems","Ineffective breathing pattern.","Anxiety — very common, compounded by the diagnosis and prognosis.","Malnutrition and fatigue.","Knowledge deficit — helping the patient understand the diagnosis and how to take care of themselves.","Collaborative care includes health promotion (smoking cessation) and anxiety reduction — much of nursing care in oncology is simply establishing rapport to help reduce a patient's anxiety.","Pneumonectomy — The entire lung is removed.","Lobectomy — Just a lobe is removed (the right lung has 3 lobes, the left has 2).","Wedge / Segmental Resection — An even smaller portion removed. Which option is used comes down to surgeon preference, depending on how the cancer has spread.","Radiation and/or chemotherapy are also often used in addition to surgery — the cancer-specific details of those are covered later, in oncology, not in this lecture.","Post-pneumonectomy assessment: expect no breath sounds on the side where the lung was removed — there's no lung there to move air. On a chest X-ray, that side will appear white instead of black, since air shows up black on X-ray and there's…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]}];
